    <item>
      <title>k3s</title>
      <link>https://book.rymcg.tech/tags/k3s/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 28 Dec 2020 00:12:00 -0600</pubDate>
      <guid>https://book.rymcg.tech/tags/k3s/</guid>
      <description>Self-Hosted Literate K3s Cluster This series of blog posts comprises a Literate Programming notebook for the BASH shell, for the purpose of bootstrapping a self-hosted K3s kubernetes cluster using GitOps (Git+DevOps) principles. Flux (v2) is a controller that runs on top of kubernetes, that will synchronize your git repositories, containing all of your kubernetes manifests (YAML), and automatically apply these changes to your cluster. With Flux, you can administer all of your infrastructure via pull request!</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>nixos-vm-template</title>
      <link>https://book.rymcg.tech/tags/nixos-vm-template/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 26 Jun 2026 12:00:00 -0600</pubDate>
      <guid>https://book.rymcg.tech/tags/nixos-vm-template/</guid>
      <description>nixos-vm-template nixos-vm-template is a tool for building and managing NixOS virtual machines with a focus on immutability and reproducibility. It supports multiple backends (libvirt for local development, Proxmox for server infrastructure) and provides a unified interface for creating, upgrading, and managing VMs.&#xA;The core design uses an immutable architecture: a read-only root filesystem containing the NixOS system, with mutable state isolated on a separate /var disk. Multiple VMs can share a single base image through QCOW2 backing files, and upgrades happen by replacing the boot image while preserving all data.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>proxmox</title>
      <link>https://book.rymcg.tech/tags/proxmox/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 26 Jun 2026 12:00:00 -0600</pubDate>
      <guid>https://book.rymcg.tech/tags/proxmox/</guid>
      <description>Proxmox Proxmox VE is an open source Virtual Machine hypervisor Operating System, built on top of Debian Linux. It has a fully programmable API, can operate as a cluster, and can behave as your own self-hosted mini cloud, for compute and storage. Proxmox excels as an agile research and development environment, making it easy to create new virtual machines, whenever you have new ideas to try, or to automate resources as part of a script.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>proxmox</title>
      <link>https://book.rymcg.tech/tags/virtual-proxmox/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 18 Nov 2023 00:01:00 -0600</pubDate>
      <guid>https://book.rymcg.tech/tags/virtual-proxmox/</guid>
      <description>Virtual Proxmox Yo dawg, you can run Proxmox inside another virtual machine, through nested virtualization. In part one: Virtual Proxmox, you will learn how to install Proxmox on any Linux computer (inside of an existing operating system). Proxmox itself will be running in a KVM virtual machine. (Or you can skip this step and install on real hardware.) On top of Proxmox, you will prepare an Ubuntu VM template, configuring the default VM size (cpu+memory+storage), and adding your SSH keys for cloud-init.</description>
    </item>
